Steve Barclay the health secretary  writing and the independent suggesting  that strikes could go on for another  three months are you with the same  opinion well I certainly hope not but  that's really in the hands of the trade  unions we want to be as reasonable as  possible Steve Barkley and other  ministers have been meeting with Union  representatives to talk to them to see  if there are ways forward to discuss  next year's pay settlement and set out  our arguments on affordability there but  I think what you see with inflation  today means that if the international  factors are beginning tentatively to  work in the right direction the worst  thing that we could do domestically  would be to significantly increase  public sector pay and then entrench  inflation in the British economy and get  into a kind of wage spiral that will be  very detrimental to the economy so we  have to approach this with great caution  because inflation is the big evil that's  the thing that's diminishing people's  savings is increasing interest rates and  mortgage rates and making life generally  harder for everyone and so we have to  approach these public sector pay  discussion  with that broader mission in mind which  is to bring down inflation by at least  50 over the course of this year and to  that end you know you can take on uh the  rail unions you can take on uh the civil  servants unions but you know when it  comes to taking on the nurses you don't  have the support of the of the British  people and and I've seen the banners  that say a nurses for life not just for  covert well we have great respect for  nurses of course we do I'll give them  all um well it's not always as simple as  that in life argument okay because what  we have to judge is not only how can we  motivate and respect nurses and there is  a serious challenge with retention and  recruitment within the NHS we have to  balance that however with General  affordability to the taxpayer what can  the NHS with its record level of  investment right now afford secondly the  point about inflation which is so  critical to everybody in this country  and thirdly how can we manage this in a  sensible and appropriate great Manner  and the way that's done is through  independent pay review bodies and there  was an independent pay review last year  which concluded that nurses should get  the pay rise that they have we accepted  their recommendations this isn't an  independent is it it is independent okay  is it not uh presided over by the Prime  Minister and lots of government  ministers are involved in deciding how  much that money is well government  ministers play a role in it in that we  submit recommendations and give a  mandate to the independent pay review  body and we're doing that now for next  year the next financial year but then  it's ultimately for that group of  experts to look at the evidence to  listen to the perspective of the  workforce of trade unions  and come to a balanced judgment isn't it  don't ministers tell that pay review  body how much is in the past well we do  say what's affordable as you'd expect  because when any business or  organization considers how much it can  afford to pay its staff it has to work  out what is in the pot if you like like  but they also take into account what's  happening in the economy where inflation  is and is likely to be over the course  of the year and the particular  challenges in that sector to do with  Recruitment and Retention and at the  moment the independent period is gearing  up to work out what it should be a fair  second for the next financial year and  that's really our Focus right now and  they will be taking account of the  inflation on the first of February and  the fact that that's a coordinate  coordinated day of action it is  absolutely and one of my  responsibilities as immigration Ministry  is to keep our borders secure and allow  passengers to flow through our airports  as efficiently as possible and so I'm  working very hard to see that if that  strike includes border Force personnel  as it did over Christmas and New Year  that we have the right response and I  hope you and your listeners will agree  that the response we put in place over  Christmas and New Year was a good one we  use 600 Armed Forces Personnel 50  percent of border staff came into work  despite the strike action and actually  our borders were as secure as usual and  flowed if anything better than they do  on a normal working day and so that's  the kind of detailed work that I'm  involved in right now so that we can try  to ensure the country works as well as  it can on that day but the key thing is  that the unions don't proceed with those  strikes because all it's going to do  well they're going to well that's a  matter for them it is we can only  prepare but what I my message to them  would be think about the impact it's  going to have on working people across  the country and on the economy we know  that times are difficult right now what  we don't want to see is the economy  harmed by you know an act of self-harm  by further strike action that makes it  harder to get to work to cross the  border and of course to get access to  crucial Public Services it can be better  not let me talk about her to you if I  may um she has been writing in oh she's  um there's an article let's say in the  telegraph today about uh um transgender  conversion vision therapy she has said  she's not actually written to MPS as yet  I believe Tory MPS but she wants the ban  on trans conversion therapy not to  criminalize parents I'm not quite sure  where she's going with that do you  understand that yes I do  we all support the abolition of  conversion therapies which are you know  malicious attempts to convince people to  live lives outside of their gender and  so Banning people from doing those kind  of courses or interventions is  absolutely the right thing but this is a  much more complex issue than perhaps it  seems at first glance what we don't want  to see happen is parents loved ones  teachers people who want to provide  support and advice to Children put off  or criminalized from doing so if you're  a young person who is trans or is  thinking through the issues related to  trans it is absolutely right that you  should be able to turn to your parents  or trusted adults for support and advice  at that time and as a parent it is  absolutely right that I could provide  that kind of advice and support to my  chart without fear or without fear of  either being criminalized or there being  some other sanction as a result of doing  so so the way in which this new law will  need to be drafted is one which strikes  that balance so that the malicious  activities are criminalized and are  consigned to history but that  well-meaning loved ones and trusted  advisors to children can provide the  support and advice that they need to do  okay and so the way we're going to  handle this as a government is to do  detailed pre-legitive scrutiny which  those colleagues in Parliament who are  rightly concerned about how this will  work in practice can contribute to and  then we will craft careful legislation  in due course we're not going to rush  into this because I think it's a  surprisingly complex issue which we need  to treat appropriately okay we're almost  out of time but I wanted to ask you  about David Carrick that disgusting  disgraceful police officer who managed  to carry out a significant number of  rapes while he worked for the  Metropolitan Police my question is  twofold who is responsible for him being  allowed to stay in his post for 18 years  and should he have his pension removed  from him well on the on the second of  those two points  um the forfeiture of the pension is a  matter for the mayor of London but we  support his efforts to remove that  pension if that is indeed what he  chooses to do you know this is one of  the most egregious cases of police  misconduct in the history of the Met  perhaps in the history of British  policing and this disgusting individual  should not benefit from his years  serving in the metropolitan police there  now needs to be a very detailed  investigation as to exactly what  happened and who might have known about  this during his years of service Sir  Mark Rowley who's leading the  Metropolitan Police is focused on that  the Home Secretary has met with him to  get explicit assurances that he will be  working now intensively to understand  the events how this could possibly have  happened for such a prolonged period of  time and what we can do to make sure it  never happens again and then secondly  we've asked Dame Elish uh Angelini to do  a comprehensive review for us about the  culture in the Police Service about  vetting and recruitment procedures why  these cases of misogyny and Prejudice  seem to happen time and again and what  we can learn from that and we're asking  every police constabulary in the country  to improve their vetting and recruitment  procedures and giving them the funding  to do so there are other important  pieces of work on going like for example  from Dame Louise Casey who I've worked  very closely with in the past have the  utmost respect for and so I hope that  this body of work there was ongoing even  before we learned about this disgusting  case will enable change to happen  finally in the met and other police  force across the country we must leave  it there lots for us to unpick  throughout the course of the program  thank you very much indeed thank you for  the time much appreciated thank you

